---
import {Image} from "astro:assets";

import infographic from "@src/assets/images/common-kestrel-infographic.jpg";
import fieldwork from "@src/assets/images/kestrel-fieldwork.jpg";
import ContactPanel from "@src/components/contact-panel/ContactPanel.astro";
import PageIntro from "@src/components/page-intro/PageIntro.astro";
import BaseLayout from "@src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Kestrels NI"
  description="Learn about Common Kestrels in Ireland, why their decline matters, and how to report a kestrel sighting."
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Kestrels NI"
    title="A familiar falcon we still need to understand."
    summary="Kez completed her PhD at Queen's University Belfast, researching the ecology of the Common Kestrel in Ireland under the supervision of Dr Paul Caplat."
    width="wide"
  />

  <section class="shell kestrel-opening">
    <figure>
      <Image
        src={fieldwork}
        alt="Kez Armstrong carefully measuring a kestrel chick during fieldwork"
        widths={[560, 840, 1120]}
        sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 48vw"
        format="avif"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Field measurements contribute to a better picture of kestrel ecology in Ireland.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Although a small, relatively easy falcon to see on the island of Ireland, sightings are rarely
        reported.
      </p>
      <p>
        They are commonly seen hovering with a splayed tail, hunting for small mammals, insects and
        occasionally small birds. They are found in a variety of open habitats, including farmland, moorland,
        coasts, wetlands, roadside verges and parks.
      </p>
      <p>
        The PhD research was completed at <a href="https://www.qub.ac.uk/">Queen's University Belfast</a>
        under the supervision of <a href="https://paulcaplat.weebly.com/">Dr Paul Caplat</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sighting-band">
    <div class="shell sighting-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Take part</p>
        <h2>Have you seen a kestrel?</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="lede">
          Help build the evidence by recording sightings where you live or while you are out and about.
        </p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a class="button button--light" href="https://www.raptormonitor.com/">
            Report via RaptorMonitor
          </a>
          <a class="text-link text-link--light" href="https://www.bto.org/our-science/projects/birdtrack">
            Use BTO BirdTrack <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section shell why-grid">
    <div class="sticky-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why this matters</p>
      <h2>Decline is a signal from the wider landscape.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Kestrels are declining on the island of Ireland. They are encountered too infrequently in Breeding
        Bird Surveys for annual trend indices to be generated in Northern Ireland. In the Republic of Ireland,
        the assessment cited by the original project reported an annual rate of decline greater than 3% per
        year.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is growing evidence that changes in land-use practices are reducing prey availability,
        increasing persecution and contributing to a decline in reproductive success. Other possible factors
        include intra-guild predation rates increasing with rises in populations of other raptors and corvids.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kestrels provide essential ecosystem services on agricultural land through pest control—eating small
        mammals—and can indicate the health of an ecosystem. By understanding what is happening to their
        population, we can gather information about the other animals that depend on this habitat for at least
        part of their life cycle.
      </p>
      <p class="source-note">
        Population context:
        <a href="https://birdwatchireland.ie/app/uploads/2019/09/BOCCI.pdf">
          Birds of Conservation Concern in Ireland
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="infographic-section">
    <div class="shell infographic-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Field guide</p>
        <h2>The Common Kestrel in Ireland.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Falco tinnunculus · Pocaire gaoithe</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <a href={infographic.src} aria-label="Open the Common Kestrel infographic at full size">
          <Image
            src={infographic}
            alt="Illustrated Common Kestrel guide; a full text transcript follows"
            widths={[720, 1080, 1440]}
            sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 60vw"
            format="webp"
          />
        </a>

        <details>
          <summary>Read the infographic transcript</summary>
          <div class="prose transcript">
            <h3>The Common Kestrel in Ireland</h3>
            <p>
              <em>Falco tinnunculus</em> · Pocaire gaoithe
            </p>
            <p>
              Once a familiar sight above fields and motorway verges, the kestrel population in the UK has
              declined by 50% in the past twenty years, and in Ireland they are declining by more than 3% per
              year. These figures reproduce the wording of the original project infographic and should be read
              in its publication context.
            </p>
            <p>
              The female has a brown head, barred back and wings, brown wing tips and a barred tail. The male
              has a grey head, black-spotted back and wings, black wing tips, a grey tail, streaked chest and
              yellow feet.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>Although a small-mammal specialist, in Ireland they depend on avian prey.</li>
              <li>
                They face pressures from landscape change such as deforestation and agricultural
                intensification.
              </li>
              <li>They lay four to six eggs and fledge three to four chicks on average.</li>
              <li>
                They have a wide geographic range; however, little is known about distribution and behaviour
                across Ireland.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              What can you do to help? Report all kestrel sightings to Raptor Study Groups and use BTO
              BirdTrack. Contact: kestrelsNI@gmail.com.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ContactPanel
    heading="Have a kestrel research or ecology question?"
    message="For research, collaboration or specialist ecological support, contact Kez directly."
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .kestrel-opening,
  .why-grid,
  .sighting-grid,
  .infographic-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 8rem);
  }

  figure {
    margin: 0;
  }

  figure :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  figcaption,
  .source-note {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--slate);
    font-size: 0.76rem;
  }

  .sighting-band {
    margin-top: clamp(4rem, 10vw, 9rem);
    padding-block: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 6rem);
    color: var(--paper);
    background: var(--teal-deep);
  }

  .sighting-grid .eyebrow {
    color: white;
  }

  .sighting-grid .lede {
    color: white;
  }

  .sighting-grid .button-row {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .sticky-heading {
    align-self: start;
  }

  .infographic-section {
    padding-block: clamp(4rem, 9vw, 8rem);
    background: var(--paper-deep);
  }

  .infographic-grid {
    grid-template-columns: 0.55fr 1.45fr;
  }

  .infographic-grid :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    box-shadow: 0 1rem 4rem rgb(32 42 48 / 0.12);
  }

  details {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  summary {
    padding-block: 1rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .transcript {
    padding-block: 0.5rem 2rem;
  }

  .transcript ul {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 761px) {
    .sticky-heading {
      position: sticky;
      top: 2rem;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .kestrel-opening,
    .why-grid,
    .sighting-grid,
    .infographic-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
